{"name":"Two Cents","hex":"#854a2e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"two cents","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One's opinion or thoughts.","A nearly worthless amount, alluding to placing a copper penny on each of the eyelids of a pauper's or slave's body before burial."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/two_cents"}],"entries":[{"title":"Australian two-cent coin","blocks":[{"text":"The two-cent coin was an Australian coin introduced in 1966. It was the second-lowest denomination until it was withdrawn from circulation in 1992. It is still counted as legal tender, but is subject to some restrictions, and two-cent coins are legal tender only up to the sum of 20 cents."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/18/Australian_2c_Coin.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_two-cent_coin"},{"title":"2 euro cent coin","blocks":[{"text":"The 2 euro cent coin (€0.02) has a value of one fiftieth of a euro and is composed of copper-plated steel. All euro coins have a common reverse and country-specific (national) obverse. The coin has been used since 2002 and was not redesigned in 2007 as were the higher-value coins."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8f/2_eurocent_common_1999.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2_euro_cent_coin"},{"title":"2","blocks":[{"text":"2 (two) is a number, numeral and digit. It is the natural number following 1 and preceding 3. It is the smallest and the only even prime number."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/1%2B2%2B5_Cents%2C_Andorra.jpg/500px-1%2B2%2B5_Cents%2C_Andorra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu